/**
 * @page host_guide Host
 *
 * The **host side** drives a USB device instead of being one. It talks USB/IP
 * itself, so it needs **no kernel driver, no `vhci` and no root** - serve a device
 * in one process and drive it from another, on any OS. The device can be virtual
 * (either library) or genuine hardware exported by a real `usbipd`.
 *
 * Three ways in, from most to least code you write:
 *
 * - @subpage host_api - the native libusb-shaped API: open a device, run
 *   control/bulk/interrupt/iso transfers.
 * - @subpage host_pyusb - code already written against pyusb drives a virtual
 *   device by loading the libusb wrapper as its backend.
 * - @subpage wrappers - an already-compiled binary (`dfu-util`, `lsusb`, a WinUSB
 *   app) runs unmodified against a virtual device.
 *
 * In USB/IP terms this side is the **client** (@ref roles "role table") - something
 * must already be serving before any host call can succeed.
 */
